# Target C-II — propagate the branch-aware `g=0.0071` gap through restored rhombus energy

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Repository base:** `639c480` (`g=0.0071` theorem and exact certificate on current main)  
**Status:** proof-only quantitative propagation; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** fully force-visible no-container/full-core Type-II branch  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The hostile-audited complement O–O rhombus-incidence theorem and its restored-energy
refinement are symbolic in the matched atomic lower bound

\[
C_0\le C:={T-q/2\over D}.
\]

The new branch-aware iterative-pruning theorem raises that input from

\[
C_{50}=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0050
\]

to

\[
\boxed{C_{71}=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0071},
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\tag{GP.1}
\]

No geometric part of the rhombus-incidence proof changes.  Substituting (GP.1) into
the already-audited scalar stack raises the safe low-`t` floor from `1.84283` to

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {q-k\over D}\ge1.84323.
}
\tag{GP.2}
\]

The new scalar crossing is

\[
1.84323535293446\ldots .
\tag{GP.3}
\]

Only the round value `1.84323` is claimed.

---

## 1. Symbolic inputs from the audited rhombus-incidence stack

Pass to a convergent Type-II subsequence and write

\[
t={q-k\over D},
\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},
\qquad
\delta={d_{out}\over D}.
\]

The Section-171/network envelope is

\[
C\le U:={9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-\frac\kappa2.
\tag{GP.4}
\]

Together with (GP.1),

\[
\boxed{
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}
\le R_{71}(t):={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_{71}.
}
\tag{GP.5}
\]

The two-bin dominant-orbit theorem is also symbolic in `U`.  Put

\[
m_2(U):={U+\sqrt{8\Delta_2-U^2}\over2},
\qquad
r(U):=U-m_2(U).
\tag{GP.6}
\]

Then the complement complete-train density is bounded by

\[
U+{t+\kappa\over2}-m_2(U),
\]

and the hostile-audited shared-retention theorem supplies retained dominant outer
occurrence mass `z` and retained complement O–O pair mass `p` with

\[
\boxed{
z+p\ge
S(t,\delta,\kappa)
:=\pi-{t+\kappa\over2}-r(U)-o(1).
}
\tag{GP.7}

The retained dominant occurrences consume directional energy at least

\[
F(z)D-o(D),
\qquad
F(z):=z-6\sin(z/6).
\tag{GP.8}

Finally, the hostile-audited complement-rhombus incidence theorem gives

\[
p(1-\delta)-{e_c\over2}
\le {r(U)^2\over2}+o(1),
\tag{GP.9}
\]

where `e_c D` is the exact terminal directional energy carried by the retained
complement pairs.  Since dominant and complement terminal occurrences are disjoint,

\[
e_c\le\delta-F(z)+o(1).
\tag{GP.10}

Equations (GP.7)--(GP.10) are exactly the restored-energy stack already audited on the
`g=0.0050` input.  The only replacement here is (GP.1).

---

## 2. Eliminate the complement population

From (GP.9)--(GP.10),

\[
p\le
{\delta-F(z)+r(U)^2\over2(1-\delta)}+o(1).
\tag{GP.11}

Combining with (GP.7), every survivor must satisfy

\[
G(z;t,\delta,\kappa)
:=z+{\delta-F(z)+r(U)^2\over2(1-\delta)}
\ge S(t,\delta,\kappa)-o(1).
\tag{GP.12}

On the interval used below, `delta<0.05` and `0<=z<=pi`, so

\[
G_z
=1-{F'(z)\over2(1-\delta)}
>0.929.
\tag{GP.13}

Thus `G` is strictly increasing.  Let `Z_*(t,delta,kappa)` be the unique threshold
solving

\[
G(Z_*;t,\delta,\kappa)=S(t,\delta,\kappa).
\tag{GP.14}

Every survivor obeys

\[
\boxed{
\delta\ge F(Z_*).
}
\tag{GP.15}

---

## 3. The full feasible triangle still reduces to its network boundary

The exact certificate covers

\[
1.84283\le t\le1.84324
\]

and gives, over the complete feasible region (GP.5),

\[
C_{71}\le U<2.012,
\qquad
0\le\delta<0.050,
\tag{GP.16}
\]

\[
0<r(U)<0.111,
\qquad
-0.062<m_2'(U)<0.
\tag{GP.17}
\]

Put

\[
r'(U)=1-m_2'(U).
\]

Then

\[
1<r'(U)<1.062.
\tag{GP.18}
\]

### Topology direction

At fixed `t,delta`, `U_kappa=-1/2`.  Since

\[
S_\kappa={r'-1\over2}=-{m_2'(U)\over2}>0
\]

and

\[
G_U={r r'\over1-\delta}>0,
\]

differentiating (GP.14) gives

\[
G_z Z_{*,\kappa}=S_\kappa+{G_U\over2}>0.
\]

Therefore `Z_*`, hence `F(Z_*)-delta`, increases with `kappa`.  The weakest point has

\[
\boxed{\kappa=0.}
\tag{GP.19}

### Detour direction

At fixed `t,kappa`, `U_delta=-2` and

\[
S_\delta=2r'<2.124.
\]

The partial derivative of `G` with respect to `delta` at fixed `z,U` is positive, so
omitting it gives an upper bound for `Z_{*,delta}`.  The certificate bounds

\[
G_U<0.125,
\qquad
G_z>0.929.
\]

Consequently

\[
Z_{*,\delta}
< {2(1.062)+2(0.125)\over0.929}
<2.56.
\tag{GP.20}

For `0<=Z_*<=pi`,

\[
F'(Z_*)\le1-{\sqrt3\over2}<0.134.
\]

Hence

\[
{d\over d\delta}[F(Z_*)-\delta]
<2.56(0.134)-1
<-0.65.
\tag{GP.21}

Thus maximal detour is the weakest point.  Combining with (GP.19) and (GP.5),

\[
\boxed{
\kappa=0,
\qquad
\delta={R_{71}(t)\over2},
\qquad
U=C_{71}.
}
\tag{GP.22}

---

## 4. Boundary scalar

Put

\[
C_0:=C_{71},
\qquad
M_0:=m_2(C_0),
\qquad
r_0:=C_0-M_0.
\]

Numerically for orientation,

\[
C_0=1.9117256137279146\ldots,
\qquad
r_0=0.0071132831811389\ldots .
\tag{GP.23}

At (GP.22),

\[
\delta_0(t)
={1\over2}\left({9t\over2}-2\pi-C_0\right),
\qquad
S_0(t)=\pi-{t\over2}-r_0.
\tag{GP.24}

Let `Z_0(t)` be the unique solution of

\[
Z_0+{\delta_0-F(Z_0)+r_0^2\over2(1-\delta_0)}
=S_0(t).
\tag{GP.25}

A survivor requires

\[
H_0(t):=F(Z_0(t))-\delta_0(t)\le0.
\tag{GP.26}

The monotonicity argument of Section 3 makes `H_0` strictly decreasing.  The independent
high-precision Decimal certificate gives

\[
H_0(1.84323)
=1.2672491576\times10^{-5}>0,
\]

\[
H_0(1.84324)
=-1.1001428615\times10^{-5}<0,
\]

and the root bracket

\[
1.84323535293446<t_*<1.84323535293447.
\tag{GP.27}

Therefore every survivor in the stated proof-only scope satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{q-k\over D}\ge1.84323.
}
\tag{GP.28}

---

## 5. Frontier effect

The gain from `g=0.0071` is not another local geometry theorem.  It strengthens the
matched all-train and strict-rhombus phase gap, which tightens the global network
budget before the outer-train incidence argument is applied.  The energy-restored
rhombus theorem then converts that `0.0021` increase in matched atomic gap into an
additional

\[
1.84323535\ldots-1.84283805\ldots
=0.00039729\ldots
\]

in the normalized `q-k` floor.

At this endpoint the complement escape remains negligible.  The next useful target is
still the dominant exact three-phase orbit itself: phase-by-phase outer terminal
routing, dominant-rhombus corridor geometry, or a simultaneous train-shear/diameter
thrackle contradiction.

No theorem-state promotion is requested.  C-II remains open.